Although the results shown in Table  1  are associated with a high standard dispersion, they seem to indicate that the fraction of austenite retained after a treatment at 400 °C and 350 °C is almost independent of the duration of the treatment. In the case of the treatment at 350 °C, it can be assumed that when the bainitic transformation is finished, the amount of retained austenite and its carbon content practically coincide with the values measured by XRD. This is because the amount of athermic martensite formed before the isothermal treatment is very low since this temperature is only 2 °C lower than the  M s , and besides the curve of cooling to room temperature does not show any martensitic transformation. But as the Fig.  5 b shows, after holding at 400 °C some amount of martensite is always formed on cooling to room temperatures. The actual amount of austenite has to be obtained by computing both contribution, and then the mean C content of austenite at such T can determined keeping in mind that 4 C γ ∗ f γ Iso = C γ ∗ f γ RT + C α ′ ∗ f α ′ RT \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
